window.AIBEMOCK={"n":3,"title":"Mock Test 3","theme":"Full syllabus","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"A third full-length paper in the same pattern, with recent amendments and landmark judgments mixed into the core subjects.","minutes":210,"neg":0,"pass":{"General / OBC":45,"SC / ST / PwD":40},"asOn":"19 November 2026","q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Common Cause v Union of India (2018), the Supreme Court held that","o":["active euthanasia by lethal injection is lawful in all cases, without safeguards","passive euthanasia and living wills are permissible, with safeguards","living wills are void in the absence of an enacted statute made by Parliament","passive euthanasia is unlawful and amounts to abetment of suicide by the doctor"],"a":1,"e":"A Constitution Bench held that the right to die with dignity is part of Article 21 and allowed passive euthanasia and advance medical directives, with guidelines issued until Parliament legislates. Active euthanasia remains unlawful. The procedure was simplified in 2023."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 320 of the CrPC (now Section 359 of the BNSS), the composition of an offence has the effect of","o":["an acquittal of the accused","a stay of the proceedings, which may later be revived","a discharge of the accused","a conviction with only a fine"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 320(8), the composition of an offence has the effect of an acquittal of the accused with whom the offence has been compounded. Some offences can be compounded without, and others only with, the leave of the court."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Which of the following is 'grievous hurt' under Section 116 of the BNS (earlier Section 320 IPC)?","o":["Any hurt that prevents the victim from his ordinary pursuits for ten days","A bruise on the arm that is painful to touch","A cut that causes bleeding from the arm","Fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth"],"a":3,"e":"The eight kinds of grievous hurt include emasculation, permanent loss of sight or hearing, loss of a limb or joint, permanent disfiguration of the head or face, fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth, any hurt endangering life, and any hurt that causes severe bodily pain or keeps the victim from his ordinary pursuits for twenty days."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"An interpleader suit under Section 88 CPC lies where","o":["the defendant is a minor, who has been represented in the suit by a next friend","two or more persons claim the same property from the plaintiff, who claims no interest in it except charges or costs","no person has made any claim whatsoever to the property that is in dispute here","the plaintiff claims to be the absolute owner of the property which is in dispute between the parties"],"a":1,"e":"An interpleader suit is filed by a person against whom conflicting claims are made by two or more persons, who is ready to hand over the property to the rightful claimant, and who has no interest in it apart from charges or costs."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 162 of the CrPC (now Section 181 of the BNSS), a statement made to a police officer during investigation can be used by the accused only","o":["as substantive evidence of the facts stated in it, in the trial of the accused","to prove the guilt of the accused person at the trial of the case","to corroborate the maker when he is examined as a witness in the case","to contradict the maker when he is examined as a witness"],"a":3,"e":"Statements recorded under Section 161 (now Section 180 BNSS) are not signed by the maker. They cannot be used for any purpose except to contradict the maker as provided under the Evidence Act, or in re-examination to explain the contradiction."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which of the following retrospective laws is NOT prohibited by Article 20(1) of the Constitution?","o":["A law that enhances the penalty for an offence already committed","A law that retrospectively imposes a tax or other civil liability","A law that makes an act an offence with effect from an earlier date","A law that imposes a greater penalty than was in force at the time of the offence"],"a":1,"e":"Article 20(1) bars conviction for an act that was not an offence when done, and the imposition of a penalty greater than that provided at the time of the offence. It applies only to criminal laws, and does not prevent retrospective civil or tax laws."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under Section 84 of the Patents Act, 1970, an application for a compulsory licence may be made after the expiry of","o":["seven years from the date of the grant of the patent","five years from the date of the grant of the patent","three years from the date of the grant of the patent","one year from the date of the grant of the patent"],"a":2,"e":"Any person interested may apply, on grounds such as the reasonable requirements of the public not being satisfied, the invention not being available at a reasonably affordable price, or the patented invention not being worked in India."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"A sentence of death passed by a Court of Session cannot be executed unless it is confirmed by","o":["the Governor of the State","the Supreme Court","the High Court","the Chief Judicial Magistrate"],"a":2,"e":"Section 366 CrPC (now Section 407 BNSS) requires the Sessions Judge to submit the proceedings to the High Court, and the sentence is not executed unless confirmed by it. The High Court may confirm, set aside, or pass any other sentence."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Social Security, 2020, a woman who has fewer than two surviving children is entitled to maternity benefit for","o":["twelve weeks","sixteen weeks","twenty-four weeks","twenty-six weeks"],"a":3,"e":"The Code provides 26 weeks, of which not more than 8 weeks may precede the expected delivery. For a woman with two or more surviving children, it is 12 weeks. The employee must have worked for at least 80 days in the preceding twelve months."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under the first proviso to Section 92 of the Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision), oral evidence to show that a written contract is invalid because of fraud is","o":["permissible, as the proviso allows proof of fraud, intimidation or illegality","permissible only if the document in question has not been registered under the law","permissible only if the other party consents to its being led in evidence","barred, since oral evidence can never vary the terms of a written contract at all"],"a":0,"e":"The general rule excludes oral evidence to contradict, vary, add to or subtract from the terms of a written contract. The provisos permit proof of any fact that invalidates the document, such as fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of due execution or want of consideration."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"A joint sitting of both Houses of Parliament under Article 108 is presided over by","o":["the senior-most member of the Lok Sabha","the Speaker of the Lok Sabha","the President of India","the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha"],"a":1,"e":"Article 108 provides for a joint sitting to resolve a deadlock on an ordinary Bill. It is presided over by the Speaker, or in his absence the Deputy Speaker, and not by the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha. Money Bills and Constitution Amendment Bills cannot be subject to a joint sitting."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Under Section 72 of the Information Technology Act, 2000, breach of confidentiality and privacy by a person who has secured access to records under the Act is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["two years, or fine up to Rs. 1 lakh, or both","one year, or fine up to Rs. 1 lakh, or both","five years, or fine up to Rs. 10 lakh, or both","three years, or fine up to Rs. 5 lakh, or both"],"a":0,"e":"Section 72 applies to persons empowered under the Act, such as officers who access electronic records, and who disclose them to another person without the consent of the person concerned."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under the proviso to Order VI Rule 17 CPC, after the commencement of trial, an application for amendment of pleadings is not allowed unless","o":["the High Court has granted leave to the party who is applying for it","the court finds that, despite due diligence, the matter could not be raised earlier","the amendment is sought before the judgment is pronounced by the court","the opposite party gives its written consent to the amendment of the pleadings"],"a":1,"e":"The proviso, added in 2002, restricts amendments after the trial has commenced. The party must show due diligence. Otherwise, amendments necessary for determining the real questions in controversy are allowed liberally."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under Section 25 of the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, the Collector must make an award within","o":["two years from the date of publication of the declaration","five years from the date of publication of the declaration","three years from the date of publication of the declaration","one year from the date of publication of the declaration"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 25, if no award is made within two years from the date of publication of the declaration under Section 19, the entire proceedings for acquisition lapse, and a fresh process must be started."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under the BCI Rules, an advocate may withdraw from an engagement once accepted","o":["at any time and without notice, if the client delays in paying his fees","only for sufficient cause and after giving reasonable notice to the client","only with the written permission of the State Bar Council concerned","only after the case has been finally decided by the trial court"],"a":1,"e":"An advocate must not withdraw from engagement without sufficient cause and reasonable notice to the client. If he withdraws, he must refund any part of the fee that has not been earned."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Secondary evidence of the contents of a document may be given where the original is","o":["in the possession of the person against whom it is to be proved, who fails to produce it after notice","in the court's own record room, which is far from the courtroom where it is needed","kept in a different district, which makes its production inconvenient for the party","in the possession of the party leading evidence, but he prefers to produce a copy"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary evidence is allowed in the cases listed in Section 65 of the Evidence Act, such as where the original is with the adverse party who does not produce it after notice, or is lost or destroyed. A party cannot choose secondary evidence for convenience."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 18 of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, a Hindu wife is NOT entitled to separate residence and maintenance if she","o":["has ceased to be a Hindu by conversion to another religion","alleges that her husband has treated her with cruelty","is able to prove that her husband has another wife living","has a husband suffering from a virulent form of leprosy"],"a":0,"e":"Section 18(2) lists grounds on which a wife may live separately and still claim maintenance, including desertion, cruelty, leprosy, another wife living, a concubine in the same house, and conversion of the husband. Section 18(3) disentitles a wife who is unchaste or ceases to be a Hindu by conversion."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"An appeal against a conviction passed by a Judicial Magistrate of the First Class lies to","o":["the Chief Judicial Magistrate","the District Magistrate","the Court of Session","the High Court"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 374(3) CrPC (now BNSS), a convicted person may appeal to the Court of Session against a conviction by a Magistrate. An appeal against a conviction by a Sessions Judge lies to the High Court."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Exception 4 to Section 101 of the BNS, culpable homicide is not murder if committed without premeditation in a sudden fight, in the heat of passion upon a sudden quarrel, and","o":["after the offender had left the scene and then returned to attack the victim","with a weapon that the offender had procured before the quarrel had even begun","without undue advantage or cruel or unusual conduct on the part of the offender","with the prior consent of the person who was killed in the course of the fight"],"a":2,"e":"The Exception requires four elements: absence of premeditation, a sudden fight, heat of passion upon a sudden quarrel, and the offender not having taken undue advantage or acted cruelly or unusually. It corresponds to Exception 4 to Section 300 IPC."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following is a function of the Bar Council of India under Section 7 of the Advocates Act, 1961?","o":["Laying down standards of professional conduct and etiquette for advocates","Drafting legislation on behalf of the Central Government","Enrolling advocates directly on the State rolls of advocates","Disciplining the judges of the High Courts for their conduct"],"a":0,"e":"Section 7 lists the BCI's functions, which include laying down standards of professional conduct, safeguarding the rights and privileges of advocates, promoting legal education, and recognising universities whose law degrees qualify for enrolment. Enrolment is done by State Bar Councils."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 15(1) of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, the property of a Hindu female dying intestate devolves first upon","o":["the heirs of her father, who take before her own children and her husband","her parents, and then upon the heirs of her husband in the next place","her sons, daughters (and predeceased children's children) and husband","the heirs of her husband, who take before her own children and her husband"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 15(1)(a), the sons, daughters (including children of predeceased son or daughter) and the husband take together. Then the heirs of the husband, her parents, and so on. Under Section 15(2), property inherited from her parents or husband follows a different order."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"Principle 15 of the Rio Declaration on Environment and Development, 1992 embodies","o":["the polluter pays principle","the principle of intergenerational equity","the public trust doctrine","the precautionary principle"],"a":3,"e":"Principle 15 states that, where there are threats of serious or irreversible damage, lack of full scientific certainty shall not be used as a reason for postponing cost-effective measures to prevent environmental degradation. The polluter pays principle is in Principle 16."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"The doctrine of indoor management in company law is derived from","o":["Foss v Harbottle (1843), the English case","Royal British Bank v Turquand (1856)","Gilford Motor Co. Ltd. v Horne (1933)","Salomon v Salomon & Co. Ltd. (1897)"],"a":1,"e":"The rule in Turquand's case protects outsiders dealing in good faith with the company, who are entitled to assume that the internal procedures required by the company's constitution have been followed. Exceptions include knowledge of irregularity, forgery and suspicion."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XXIII Rule 3 CPC, a compromise of a suit, to be recorded and made a decree, must be","o":["approved by the Collector of the district concerned","compulsorily registered under the Registration Act, 1908","lawful, in writing, and signed by the parties","oral, and recorded in the order sheet by the court"],"a":2,"e":"Rule 3 requires proof that the suit has been adjusted wholly or in part by a lawful agreement or compromise in writing and signed by the parties. The court then records it and passes a decree in terms of it."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"The inherent powers of the court under Section 151 CPC","o":["can be exercised only by the High Court, and not by any subordinate court","confer a right to appeal against any interim order passed by the court","cannot override an express provision, or be used where the Code gives a remedy","can be used in all cases, whether or not the Code has provided a specific remedy"],"a":2,"e":"Section 151 saves the court's inherent power to make orders necessary for the ends of justice or to prevent abuse of its process. It does not confer substantive rights, and cannot be invoked where the Code provides a specific remedy."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"In the Supreme Court of India, a petition or appeal on behalf of a litigant can be filed only by","o":["an Advocate-on-Record","any advocate enrolled on a State roll","the Registrar of the Supreme Court","any Senior Advocate designated by the Court"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Supreme Court Rules, only an Advocate-on-Record, who has passed the AoR examination, may file matters on behalf of a party. A party may also appear and argue in person. Other advocates may argue if instructed by an AoR."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Which of the following can be validly transferred under Section 6 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882?","o":["A mere right to sue","A spes successionis (a chance of inheriting)","A right of re-entry for breach of a condition","A lessee's interest in the leased property"],"a":3,"e":"Section 6 bars transfer of a spes successionis, a right of re-entry, an easement apart from the dominant heritage, a mere right to sue, a public office, stipends and pensions, and interests restricted to personal enjoyment. A lessee's interest is transferable."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under the proviso to Section 106(1) of the BNS, a registered medical practitioner who causes death by a negligent act while performing a medical procedure is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["two years, and fine","seven years, and fine","five years, and fine","one year, and fine"],"a":0,"e":"Section 106(1) BNS punishes causing death by negligence with up to five years. The proviso reduces the maximum to two years for a registered medical practitioner, in line with Jacob Mathew v State of Punjab (2005), which required gross negligence for criminal liability of doctors."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"In criminal proceedings, evidence that the accused has a bad character is irrelevant, except","o":["where the Public Prosecutor requests the court to admit such evidence of it","in reply to evidence of his good character, or where it is a fact in issue","where the accused has been previously arrested by the police at any time","in all cases where the offence alleged against him is of a very serious kind"],"a":1,"e":"Evidence of good character is always relevant in criminal cases. Evidence of bad character is relevant only to rebut it, or where bad character is itself a fact in issue. Previous convictions are relevant for sentence."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"The doctrine of lis pendens under Section 52 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 is based on the principle that","o":["nothing new should be introduced during litigation","a buyer must beware of defects in the property which he has bought","an interpretation which gives effect to a deed is preferred to one that defeats it","no one can transfer a better title to another than he himself has"],"a":0,"e":"'Pendente lite nihil innovetur'. A transfer of the property in dispute during the pendency of a suit does not affect the rights of the parties to the suit under any decree. The transferee is bound by the result, even without notice."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 77 of the BNS, the punishment for voyeurism on a first conviction is","o":["imprisonment of one to three years on a first conviction, and fine","fine only on a first conviction, as the offence is compoundable by the victim","imprisonment up to six months on a first conviction, or fine, or both","imprisonment of three to seven years on a first conviction, and fine"],"a":0,"e":"Section 77 BNS corresponds to Section 354C IPC. It is punishable on first conviction with one to three years and fine, and on a second or subsequent conviction with three to seven years and fine."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In L. Chandra Kumar v Union of India (1997), the Supreme Court held that the decisions of administrative tribunals are","o":["appealable directly to the Supreme Court in every case, without any exception","reviewable only by the President of India, on a petition made to him","final, and immune from any review by the High Court in any case at all","subject to judicial review by the High Court under Articles 226 and 227"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that judicial review is part of the basic structure, and tribunal decisions are subject to the High Court's jurisdiction under Articles 226 and 227, before a Division Bench of the High Court having jurisdiction over the tribunal."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 13 CPC, a foreign judgment is NOT conclusive as to any matter directly adjudicated if","o":["it is consistent with the law in force in India","it has not been given on the merits of the case","it was obtained after due notice to the parties","it was pronounced by a court of competent jurisdiction"],"a":1,"e":"Section 13 lists six exceptions: not pronounced by a competent court, not on the merits, founded on a wrong view of international law or refusal to recognise Indian law, opposed to natural justice, obtained by fraud, and sustaining a claim founded on a breach of Indian law."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Under Section 67A of the Information Technology Act, 2000, publishing or transmitting sexually explicit material in electronic form is punishable on a first conviction with imprisonment up to five years and fine up to","o":["Rs. 10 lakh","Rs. 5 lakh","Rs. 25 lakh","Rs. 1 lakh"],"a":0,"e":"Section 67A provides up to five years and fine up to Rs. 10 lakh on first conviction, and up to seven years and fine up to Rs. 10 lakh on a subsequent conviction. Section 67 deals with obscene material, with a fine up to Rs. 5 lakh on the first conviction."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XXXVIII Rule 5 CPC, a court may direct attachment before judgment if the defendant is about to dispose of his property with intent to","o":["obstruct or delay the execution of any decree that may be passed","avoid appearing before the court on the date fixed for hearing of the suit","settle a family dispute with his relatives outside the court of law","avoid payment of the court fees that are due on the written statement"],"a":0,"e":"Attachment before judgment is an extraordinary power. The plaintiff must show prima facie that the defendant is about to dispose of or remove the property, or leave the local limits, with intent to obstruct or delay execution of a decree."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Decisions of the GST Council are taken by a majority of not less than","o":["one-half of the weighted votes of the members present and voting","four-fifths of the weighted votes of the members present and voting","two-thirds of the weighted votes of the members present and voting","three-fourths of the weighted votes of the members present and voting"],"a":3,"e":"Under Article 279A(9), the vote of the Central Government has a weightage of one-third, and the votes of all the State Governments together have a weightage of two-thirds of the total votes cast. The Union Finance Minister is the Chairperson."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Article 136 of the Constitution, which empowers the Supreme Court to grant special leave to appeal, does NOT apply to","o":["an order passed by a tribunal constituted under a Central statute of Parliament","an interlocutory order passed by a High Court in a case that is still pending","any judgment or order of a court or tribunal under any law relating to the Armed Forces","a judgment or order of a High Court refusing to grant a certificate of appeal under Article 134A"],"a":2,"e":"Article 136(2) excludes judgments, sentences and orders passed by courts or tribunals under any law relating to the Armed Forces. Special leave is a discretionary power, which can be exercised against any other court or tribunal in India."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 96 of the Companies Act, 2013, every company other than a One Person Company must hold each subsequent Annual General Meeting within","o":["six months from the closing of the financial year","twelve months from the closing of the financial year","nine months from the closing of the financial year","three months from the closing of the financial year"],"a":0,"e":"The first AGM must be held within nine months of the closing of the first financial year. Subsequent AGMs must be held within six months of the close of each financial year, and not more than fifteen months may elapse between two AGMs."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 13 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, as amended in 2006, truth can be a valid defence to a charge of contempt if","o":["the justification is in public interest and the plea is made bona fide","the statement is made by an advocate who is on record in the case","the statement is true, whatever the motive of the person who made it","the court is willing to allow the defence at its own discretion alone"],"a":0,"e":"The 2006 amendment made 'justification by truth' a defence only if it is in the public interest and the request to invoke it is bona fide. Fair criticism of the merits of a judicial decision and fair reports of proceedings are also protected."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 78 of the BNS, the punishment for the offence of stalking on a first conviction is imprisonment up to","o":["one year, and fine","three years, and fine","seven years, and fine","five years, and fine"],"a":1,"e":"Section 78 BNS corresponds to Section 354D IPC. A first conviction is punishable with imprisonment up to three years and fine, and a second or subsequent conviction with imprisonment up to five years and fine."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 10A of the Indian Divorce Act, 1869 (inserted in 2001), a petition for divorce by mutual consent may be presented if the parties have been living separately for","o":["one year or more","two years or more","five years or more","three years or more"],"a":1,"e":"Section 10A applies to Christians and requires that the parties have been living separately for two years or more and have not been able to live together. This is different from the one year of separation under the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"In Satyabrata Ghose v Mugneeram Bangur & Co. (1954), the Supreme Court held that the doctrine of frustration in India","o":["lies outside Section 56, and is based on the English common law alone in India","applies only where performance has become physically impossible in the strict sense","is within Section 56 of the Contract Act, and covers practical impossibility","applies where performance becomes merely more expensive for the promisor to do"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that 'impossible' in Section 56 is used in the sense of impracticable and useless from the point of view of the object and purpose of the parties. Mere hardship or increased cost does not frustrate a contract."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 3 of the Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961, giving or taking of dowry is punishable with imprisonment of not less than five years and fine of not less than","o":["Rs. 10,000, or the value of the dowry, whichever is more","Rs. 25,000, or the value of the dowry, whichever is more","Rs. 5,000, or the value of the dowry, whichever is more","Rs. 15,000, or the value of the dowry, whichever is more"],"a":3,"e":"Section 3(1) provides imprisonment for not less than five years and a fine of not less than Rs. 15,000 or the value of dowry, whichever is more. Gifts given at marriage without demand are exempted if listed as per the Rules."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"According to Savigny, possession consists of","o":["legal title and registration of the property","corpus possessionis alone, without any intention","corpus possessionis and animus domini","animus domini alone, without any physical control"],"a":2,"e":"For Savigny, possession requires physical control (corpus) and the intention to hold as an owner (animus domini). Ihering, on the other hand, argued that possession is protected as a means to protect ownership and requires only corpus."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"The essential difference between wrongful restraint and wrongful confinement under the BNS is that in wrongful confinement the person is","o":["obstructed in one direction while free to move in the others","prevented from proceeding beyond certain circumscribing limits","subjected to voluntary hurt by the offender","taken away from the keeping of a lawful guardian"],"a":1,"e":"Wrongful restraint (Section 126) is obstructing a person from proceeding in any direction in which he has a right to proceed. Wrongful confinement (Section 127) is wrongfully restraining a person in such a manner as to prevent him from proceeding beyond certain circumscribing limits."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 105 of the BNSS, the process of search and seizure of property is required to be recorded through","o":["two independent local witnesses alone, without any recording","audio-video electronic means, preferably a mobile phone","a handwritten panchnama signed by the police officer alone","photographs taken by the investigating officer alone"],"a":1,"e":"Section 105 requires audio-video recording of the search and seizure process, preferably on a mobile phone, and the recording must be forwarded without delay to the District Magistrate or Chief Judicial Magistrate."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, the compensation payable for the land acquired is determined by","o":["the Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Authority","the Tehsildar of the area, on his own enquiry","the High Court having jurisdiction over the area","the Collector, who passes an award"],"a":3,"e":"Under Section 27, the Collector determines the market value of the land after hearing the interested parties. A party aggrieved by the award may seek a reference to the Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Authority."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 133 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, a surety is discharged by","o":["any delay of one month by the creditor in suing the principal debtor at all","any variance, made without his consent, in the terms of the contract he guaranteed","the death of the creditor before the debt has been fully repaid by the debtor","any change in the office of the manager of the principal debtor concerned"],"a":1,"e":"Any variance in the terms of the contract between the principal debtor and the creditor, made without the surety's consent, discharges the surety as to transactions subsequent to the variance. This follows because the surety undertook liability on the original terms."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Sir Henry Maine's famous statement on the movement of progressive societies is that it has been","o":["from custom to command","from fact to norm","from status to contract","from contract to status"],"a":2,"e":"In 'Ancient Law', Maine, a leading figure of the Historical school, observed that the movement of progressive societies has been from status to contract, as individual freedom replaced family-based legal relations."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Which of the following is entitled to free legal services under Section 12 of the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, irrespective of his means?","o":["A trader who is facing a civil dispute","A member of a Scheduled Caste or Scheduled Tribe","A company director who is accused of fraud","A landlord who is seeking eviction of a tenant"],"a":1,"e":"Section 12 entitles a member of an SC or ST, a victim of trafficking, a woman or child, a person with disability, a victim of a disaster, an industrial workman, a person in custody, and a person whose annual income is below the prescribed limit, to free legal services."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In T.M.A. Pai Foundation v State of Karnataka (2002), an eleven-judge Bench held that linguistic and religious minorities under Article 30 are to be determined","o":["district-wise, taking the district as the unit","country-wide, taking India as the unit","on the basis of the institution's location alone","State-wise, taking the State as the unit"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that, since States are organised on linguistic lines, linguistic minorities are determined with the State as the unit. For religious minorities, the same principle applies, as State laws regulating education are involved."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"A decree passed in a representative suit under Order I Rule 8 CPC binds","o":["only those persons who appear before the court after notice is issued","only the persons who are named as plaintiffs in the suit before the court","only the defendant in the suit, and no other persons at all","all persons on whose behalf, or for whose benefit, the suit is instituted"],"a":3,"e":"Rule 8 allows one or more persons, with the permission of the court, to sue or be sued on behalf of all having the same interest, provided notice is given to all persons so interested. Order I Rule 8(6) makes the decree binding on all of them."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"The examination of an accused by the court under Section 313 of the CrPC (now Section 351 of the BNSS) is","o":["not on oath, and no penalty follows if he refuses to answer or answers falsely","conducted by the Public Prosecutor, on behalf of the court and the State","on oath, as in the case of any other witness who is examined in the trial","possible only after the defence has closed its evidence in the trial"],"a":0,"e":"The accused is not administered an oath, and his answers are meant to enable him to explain the circumstances appearing against him in the evidence. Questions must be put on every incriminating circumstance, otherwise the omission can vitiate the trial."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"A Chief Judicial Magistrate may pass any sentence authorised by law, except a sentence of death, life imprisonment, or imprisonment exceeding","o":["ten years","three years","seven years","five years"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 29(2) CrPC (and the corresponding BNSS provision), the CJM can pass any sentence other than death, life imprisonment or imprisonment of more than seven years."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Under the golden rule of interpretation, a court may depart from the literal meaning of words only to avoid","o":["a result that the judge considers to be harsh on the person accused","any inconvenience to the party who is bound by the provisions of the statute","a conflict with the opinion of the members of the legislature concerned","absurdity, repugnancy or inconsistency within the statute"],"a":3,"e":"The golden rule modifies the grammatical and ordinary sense of words to the extent necessary to avoid absurdity or inconsistency, but no further. It is a limited departure from the literal rule."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In M. Nagaraj v Union of India (2006), the Supreme Court upheld the validity of Article 16(4A) but held that the State must first show","o":["a recommendation of the National Commission for Backward Classes in writing","quantifiable data on backwardness, inadequacy of representation, and efficiency","that the reservation proposed will exceed fifty per cent of the posts in the cadre","approval of the proposal by a two-thirds majority in both Houses of Parliament"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the State is not bound to provide reservation in promotions, but if it wishes to do so, it must collect quantifiable data on the backwardness of the class, on the inadequacy of its representation, and ensure that administrative efficiency under Article 335 is not affected."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"In a civil case, the mere filing of an appeal against a decree","o":["operates as a stay until the appeal is admitted by the court","stays all the proceedings in the trial court in every case","automatically stays the execution of the decree for thirty days","does not by itself operate as a stay of the decree under appeal"],"a":3,"e":"Order XLI Rule 5 provides that an appeal does not operate as a stay of proceedings under a decree, and the appellate court may order stay only for sufficient cause. The applicant must show that he will suffer substantial loss if a stay is not granted."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 64(1) of the BNS, the minimum punishment for rape (other than the aggravated forms) is","o":["rigorous imprisonment of not less than seven years, extendable to life, and fine","rigorous imprisonment of not less than ten years, extendable to life, and fine","rigorous imprisonment of not less than five years, extendable to ten years, and fine","rigorous imprisonment up to ten years only, and fine at the court's discretion"],"a":1,"e":"Section 64(1) BNS corresponds to Section 376(1) IPC after the 2013 amendment, and provides a minimum of ten years, extendable to life, and fine. Section 65 provides higher punishments for rape of women under sixteen and twelve."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Which jurist defined law as 'a command of the sovereign backed by sanction'?","o":["Leon Duguit","Rudolf von Ihering","John Austin","Henry Maine"],"a":2,"e":"Austin's imperative theory, in 'The Province of Jurisprudence Determined', treats law as a command issued by a determinate sovereign, backed by a sanction, which is habitually obeyed. Critics said it fails to explain customary and international law."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 311 of the CrPC (now Section 348 of the BNSS), a court may summon or recall any person as a witness at any stage of the trial if","o":["the charge has not yet been framed against the accused person","the prosecution gives its consent to his being summoned as a witness","the defence has closed its evidence in the case before the court","his evidence appears to be essential to the just decision of the case"],"a":3,"e":"Section 311 confers wide power on the court to summon any person as a witness, or recall and re-examine any person already examined, if the evidence appears essential to the just decision. The power must be exercised judiciously, not to fill lacunae in the prosecution case."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"A private individual can sue for a public nuisance only if he proves","o":["particular damage to himself, over and above the public's","that he is a resident of the locality in which the nuisance is said to exist","that the nuisance has continued for a period of more than one full year","that the public authorities have refused to act on his written complaint"],"a":0,"e":"A public nuisance is primarily a crime, and a private action lies only if the plaintiff suffers special damage which is direct and substantial and different in kind from that suffered by the public at large."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 21 CPC, an objection to the place of suing will be entertained by an appellate or revisional court only if it was taken","o":["for the first time in the High Court on a second appeal under Section 100","by the plaintiff alone, and not by the defendant who has been sued","at the earliest opportunity, before issues are settled, and has led to a failure of justice","after the judgment has been pronounced by the trial court on the merits"],"a":2,"e":"Section 21 requires objections to territorial jurisdiction (and pecuniary jurisdiction) to be taken at the earliest possible opportunity, and in cases where issues are settled, on or before such settlement. They must also have resulted in a failure of justice."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 351(3) of the BNS, criminal intimidation by threat to cause death or grievous hurt is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["two years, or fine, or both","ten years, or fine, or both","five years, or fine, or both","seven years, or fine, or both"],"a":3,"e":"Section 351 BNS corresponds to Sections 503 and 506 IPC. Criminal intimidation is punishable with up to two years, but where the threat is to cause death or grievous hurt, the punishment extends to seven years."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 24A of the Advocates Act, 1961, a person convicted of an offence involving moral turpitude is disqualified from enrolment, but the disqualification ceases after how long from his release?","o":["five years from the date of his release","ten years from the date of his release","two years from the date of his release","one year from the date of his release"],"a":2,"e":"Section 24A disqualifies a person convicted of an offence involving moral turpitude, or under the Protection of Civil Rights Act (untouchability), from being enrolled. The disqualification ceases two years after his release."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 45 of the Advocates Act, 1961, a person who practises in a court or before an authority while not being entitled to do so is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["six months","two years","one year","three months"],"a":0,"e":"Section 45 punishes illegal practice with imprisonment up to six months. The section is aimed at persons who practise law without being enrolled as advocates."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"After the Constitution (44th Amendment) Act, 1978, the right to property is","o":["a Directive Principle that cannot be enforced in any court","a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(f), subject to restrictions","a fundamental right under Article 31, with a right to compensation","a constitutional right under Article 300A, and not a fundamental right"],"a":3,"e":"The 44th Amendment omitted Articles 19(1)(f) and 31 and inserted Article 300A, which says no person shall be deprived of property save by authority of law. Property is now a constitutional right, and the remedy lies under Article 226 and not Article 32."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 145 of the Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision), to contradict a witness by his previous written statement, his attention must be drawn","o":["to the date of the statement made, and to no other part of it","to nothing at all, as no notice to the witness is necessary in law","to the parts of the statement that are to be used for contradicting him","to the entire statement, including the portions which are wholly irrelevant"],"a":2,"e":"The witness's attention must be drawn to the parts of the earlier statement used for contradiction, before the writing can be proved. The rule ensures that the witness has an opportunity to explain the contradiction."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 151 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, the standard of care required of a bailee is that of","o":["a man of ordinary prudence caring for his own goods of the same bulk and value","liability only where the bailee has been shown to be grossly negligent in care","the utmost care that a professional bailee could possibly exercise in the trade","absolute liability for any loss that occurs, whatever its cause may have been"],"a":0,"e":"Section 151 sets an objective standard of ordinary prudence, and Section 152 provides that, in the absence of a special contract, the bailee is not responsible for loss despite such care. A common carrier has a stricter liability under the Carriers Act."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 195 of the CrPC, cognizance of offences relating to false evidence committed in connection with a proceeding in a court can be taken only on","o":["a police report filed under Section 173","a written complaint made by that court","a private complaint made by any person","a sanction of the High Court on a petition"],"a":1,"e":"Section 195(1)(b) bars cognizance of offences such as giving false evidence in a judicial proceeding except on the complaint in writing of the court concerned, or of a court to which it is subordinate. This prevents private persons from initiating perjury prosecutions."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Under Article 53 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969, a treaty that conflicts with a peremptory norm of general international law (jus cogens) is","o":["voidable at the option of the injured State","valid, but only between the original parties","valid until the International Court of Justice declares otherwise","void"],"a":3,"e":"A peremptory norm is accepted and recognised by the international community of States as a whole as one from which no derogation is permitted. Examples include the prohibition of genocide, slavery and aggression."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"The Environment Impact Assessment Notification, 2006 was issued under the powers conferred by","o":["the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974","the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986","the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980","the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972"],"a":1,"e":"The notification was issued under Section 3 of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 and the Environment (Protection) Rules, 1986. It requires prior environmental clearance for specified projects."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In Motilal Padampat Sugar Mills v State of Uttar Pradesh (1979), the Supreme Court held that the doctrine of promissory estoppel","o":["requires a registered written contract that has been made with the Government","can be enforced against the Government, subject to public interest","cannot be invoked against the Government in any circumstance, whatever the facts","applies only against private parties, and never against any public bodies at all"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the doctrine applies against the Government, which cannot claim immunity on the basis of executive necessity. It is not defeated by lack of consideration, though the Government may show that public interest requires it to go back on the promise."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Talaq-e-ahsan under Muslim law consists of","o":["a written declaration made by the wife and sent by post to her husband","three pronouncements made in a single sitting, without any interval between them","a pronouncement made during the period of the wife's menstruation, in anger","a single pronouncement in a period of tuhr, followed by abstinence during iddat"],"a":3,"e":"Talaq-e-ahsan is regarded as the most approved form, since it is revocable during the iddat period by resumption of cohabitation. The talaq becomes irrevocable only if the iddat expires without revocation."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"An admission made by a party to a proceeding is","o":["inadmissible unless the maker repeats it on oath before the court of law","relevant against the maker, though not conclusive proof of what is admitted","conclusive proof of the matter admitted, as against the person who made it","admissible only in favour of the person who has made the admission"],"a":1,"e":"Admissions are the best evidence against their maker, but they are not conclusive proof and may be explained or rebutted. They may operate as an estoppel only where the conditions for estoppel are satisfied."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Stamp duties on bills of exchange, cheques and promissory notes are","o":["levied by the State Governments, and then shared with the Union in a fixed ratio","levied and collected by the local bodies alone, within their own local limits","levied and collected by the Union Government, and retained by it for its own use","levied by the Union, but collected and appropriated by the States concerned"],"a":3,"e":"Entry 91 of the Union List covers the rates of stamp duty on these instruments, and Article 268 provides that such duties are levied by the Government of India but collected and appropriated by the States in which they are leviable."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 23 of the Registration Act, 1908, a document (other than a will) must be presented for registration within","o":["six months from the date of its execution","two months from the date of its execution","four months from the date of its execution","one month from the date of its execution"],"a":2,"e":"The period is four months from execution. Under Section 25, the Registrar may accept a document presented after the time-limit on payment of a fine, if the delay is not more than four months beyond the period."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which decision of the Supreme Court recognised the right to a speedy trial as part of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21?","o":["Hussainara Khatoon v State of Bihar (1979)","Bandhua Mukti Morcha v Union of India (1984)","Sunil Batra v Delhi Administration (1978)","Sheela Barse v State of Maharashtra (1983)"],"a":0,"e":"In Hussainara Khatoon, which involved thousands of undertrial prisoners in Bihar jails, the Court held that a reasonable, fair and just procedure under Article 21 requires a speedy trial. It also led to the recognition of the right to free legal aid."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 114, Illustration (g) of the Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision), if a party withholds evidence that is in his power to produce, the court may presume that","o":["the evidence is inadmissible, and so cannot be taken into account","the evidence, if it had been produced, would have been favourable to him","the evidence, if produced, would be unfavourable to him","the evidence never existed in any form, and can never be produced"],"a":2,"e":"The court may presume that evidence which could be and is not produced would, if produced, be unfavourable to the person who withholds it. It is a presumption of fact, and depends on the circumstances of the case."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"An action for passing off in trade mark law is available even for","o":["only the name of a company that is registered under the Companies Act","only a registered trade mark, which has been duly renewed by its proprietor","only a well-known mark that has been registered abroad in some other country","an unregistered trade mark that has acquired goodwill in the market"],"a":3,"e":"Section 27(2) of the Trade Marks Act, 1999 preserves the common law remedy of passing off. The plaintiff must prove goodwill, misrepresentation by the defendant, and damage, the 'classical trinity' of passing off."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 44 of the Advocates Act, 1961, a Disciplinary Committee may review its own order within","o":["sixty days of the date of the order","thirty days of the date of the order","ninety days of the date of the order","one hundred and twenty days of the date of the order"],"a":0,"e":"Section 44 allows the Disciplinary Committee of a State Bar Council or the Bar Council of India to review any order made by it, on its own motion or otherwise, within 60 days of the order."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 173(1) of the BNSS, where information about a cognizable offence is given electronically, it must be signed by the informant within","o":["fifteen days of its being given","three days of its being given","seven days of its being given","twenty-four hours of its being given"],"a":1,"e":"BNSS permits e-FIRs. The police officer begins to act after the informant signs within three days. BNSS also provides for 'zero FIR', which can be registered at any police station irrespective of jurisdiction."},{"s":"Limitation Law","q":"Under the Limitation Act, 1963, the period of limitation for an appeal to the High Court from a decree or order of a subordinate court is","o":["one hundred and twenty days from the date of the decree or order appealed against","thirty days from the date of the decree or order appealed against","ninety days from the date of the decree or order appealed against","sixty days from the date of the decree or order appealed against"],"a":2,"e":"Article 116 prescribes 90 days for appeals to the High Court, and 30 days for appeals to any other court. The time requisite for obtaining a certified copy of the decree is excluded under Section 12."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 13(1)(ib) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, desertion is a ground for divorce if the respondent has deserted the petitioner for a continuous period of not less than","o":["three years immediately preceding the presentation of the petition","one year immediately preceding the presentation of the petition","two years immediately preceding the presentation of the petition","five years immediately preceding the presentation of the petition"],"a":2,"e":"Desertion requires the factum of separation and the animus deserendi, without the consent and without reasonable cause. The period of two years must be continuous and immediately precede the petition."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"The evidence of a witness who has been declared hostile","o":["is not wholly effaced, and the credible portion may be relied on","can be relied on only after the witness is re-examined by the court itself","can be relied on only by the party who called the witness to give evidence","is wholly inadmissible, and must be ignored by the court in every case at all"],"a":0,"e":"In Bhagwan Singh v State of Haryana (1976) and other decisions, the Supreme Court held that the testimony of a hostile witness is not completely effaced. The portion that is otherwise reliable can be used by the prosecution or the defence, with caution."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 25 CPC, the power to transfer a suit from a court in one State to a court in another State is exercised by","o":["the Chief Justice of the High Court of the State to which it is transferred","the Central Government, on the advice of the Law Commission","the Supreme Court","the High Court of the State from which the suit is transferred"],"a":2,"e":"Section 25, as substituted in 2002, empowers the Supreme Court to transfer any suit, appeal or other proceeding from a High Court or other civil court in one State to a High Court or civil court in another State, on the application of a party."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"A proviso to a section of a statute ordinarily","o":["repeats the main enactment in other words","carves out an exception from the main enactment","enlarges the scope of the main enactment","operates as an independent enactment on a new subject"],"a":1,"e":"The normal function of a proviso is to except something out of the enacting part, or to qualify its generality. Courts look at the substance, and a proviso may sometimes be a substantive provision."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 14 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, any property possessed by a Hindu female is held by her as","o":["a limited owner, with only a life interest","a trustee for the benefit of her husband's heirs","a limited owner until she remarries or dies","a full owner, and not as a limited owner"],"a":3,"e":"Section 14(1) converts the limited estate of a Hindu woman into an absolute estate, whether the property was acquired before or after the Act (V. Tulasamma v Sesha Reddy, 1977). Section 14(2) preserves restrictions where a grant itself creates a restricted estate."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 109, the Rajya Sabha must return a Money Bill transmitted by the Lok Sabha with its recommendations within","o":["seven days from the date of its receipt","fourteen days from the date of its receipt","six weeks from the date of its receipt","thirty days from the date of its receipt"],"a":1,"e":"If the Rajya Sabha does not return a Money Bill within 14 days, it is deemed passed by both Houses in the form passed by the Lok Sabha. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject the Rajya Sabha's recommendations."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"A stipulation in a mortgage deed that the mortgagor shall not redeem the property after a fixed period is","o":["valid, if both of the parties have agreed to it","valid, if the deed has been duly registered","void, being a clog on the equity of redemption","valid in a usufructuary mortgage alone"],"a":2,"e":"Under the principle 'once a mortgage, always a mortgage', any condition which prevents or fetters the mortgagor's right to redeem on payment of the dues is a clog on redemption and void under Section 60."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Hanafi law, the mother is entitled to the custody (hizanat) of her minor son until he attains the age of","o":["twelve years","five years","fifteen years","seven years"],"a":3,"e":"Under Hanafi law, the mother has a right to custody of a son until seven years and of a daughter until puberty. The welfare of the child is, however, the paramount consideration in custody disputes."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 90 of the Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision), a presumption as to the genuineness of a document arises when it is","o":["more than thirty years old and produced from proper custody","more than twenty years old and filed before a notary","more than fifty years old and registered with the Registrar","more than ten years old and produced by any person"],"a":0,"e":"The court may presume the signature and handwriting of a document more than 30 years old, produced from custody which the court considers proper, to be genuine. This is a presumption of fact, and not one of law."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 9 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, a party may apply to the court for interim measures","o":["only during the arbitral proceedings, and not after the award has been made","only before the arbitral proceedings have commenced, and at no later stage at all","only after the award has been enforced by the court having jurisdiction","before or during arbitral proceedings, or after the award but before enforcement"],"a":3,"e":"Section 9 allows interim measures such as protection of the subject-matter, appointment of a guardian, or an interim injunction, at any of these stages. Where the court grants such relief before commencement, arbitration must commence within 90 days."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"The 'cab-rank rule' in the Bar Council of India Rules, which requires an advocate to accept a brief, means that an advocate is bound to accept any brief","o":["only if the client agrees to pay the entire fee in advance of the hearing","from any person who approaches him, whatever the fee and whatever the court may be","in the courts where he practises, at a fee suited to his standing, subject to special circumstances","only from clients who have been recommended to him by another senior advocate"],"a":2,"e":"Rule 11 of the BCI Rules (Standards of Professional Conduct, Duty to the Court) requires an advocate to accept briefs in the courts or tribunals where he practises, at a fee consistent with his standing and the nature of the case. Special circumstances may justify refusal."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under the Sale of Goods Act, 1930, an unpaid seller's right of stoppage in transit arises when the buyer becomes","o":["unwilling to accept delivery at the port","late in the payment of the freight charges","insolvent","dissatisfied with the quality of the goods"],"a":2,"e":"Section 50 gives the unpaid seller who has parted with possession the right to stop the goods in transit if the buyer becomes insolvent, and to resume possession until the price is paid. It is a right in addition to the lien and the right of resale."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 12(5) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, a person whose relationship with the parties falls under the Seventh Schedule is","o":["ineligible only if he is related to the arbitrator who was first named by the parties","eligible, if he has made a disclosure to the parties before his appointment as arbitrator","eligible, unless the opposite party challenges him within a period of fifteen days from appointment","ineligible to be appointed as arbitrator, unless the parties waive it in writing after the dispute arises"],"a":3,"e":"In TRF Ltd. v Energo Engineering Projects (2017), the Court held that a person who is ineligible cannot nominate an arbitrator. The waiver must be by an express agreement in writing after disputes have arisen."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"The defence of volenti non fit injuria","o":["cannot ordinarily be raised against a rescuer injured in saving a person endangered by the defendant's negligence","can be raised only in cases of defamation and never in cases of negligence at all","always succeeds, whenever the plaintiff had knowledge of the danger which he faced and entered into","applies only where the plaintiff has signed a written contract accepting the risk of harm"],"a":0,"e":"In Haynes v Harwood (1935), the court held that a rescuer does not voluntarily assume the risk, since he acts under a moral duty. The defence requires both knowledge of the risk and a free and voluntary agreement to bear it."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Wages, 2019, wages of a monthly-paid employee must be paid before the expiry of the","o":["fifteenth day of the succeeding month","seventh day of the succeeding month","fifth day of the succeeding month","tenth day of the succeeding month"],"a":1,"e":"Section 17 of the Code on Wages requires wages to be paid before the seventh day of the following month for monthly-paid employees, on the last day of the week for weekly-paid employees, and on the same day for daily-paid employees."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 85 of the Constitution, the maximum interval permissible between the last sitting of Parliament in one session and its first sitting in the next session is","o":["one year","three months","four months","six months"],"a":3,"e":"Article 85(1) requires the President to summon each House so that six months do not intervene between its last sitting in one session and its first sitting in the next. Parliament must therefore meet at least twice a year."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, every industrial establishment employing how many workers must have a Grievance Redressal Committee?","o":["ten or more workers","fifty or more workers","twenty or more workers","one hundred or more workers"],"a":2,"e":"Section 4 of the Code requires establishments with 20 or more workers to constitute one or more Grievance Redressal Committees, with an equal number of employer and worker representatives. It must settle grievances within 30 days."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"The judges of the International Court of Justice are elected for a term of","o":["nine years, and are eligible for re-election","twelve years, and are eligible for re-election","five years, and are not eligible for re-election","six years, and are not eligible for re-election"],"a":0,"e":"The Court has fifteen judges, elected by the UN General Assembly and the Security Council voting independently. One-third of the Court is elected every three years."}]};
